Shawn Kearley <skearley@newfoundlandpower.com> typed:
>
> When the system is creating the disk devices it makes reference to
> DSK1, DSK2, DSK3, and DSK4.  there is no reference to DSK0, the
> original boot disk id, and the new disk DSK4 is created.  I then get
> the errors that the root device does not match the linked device.
>
> How do I go about configuring the system so that it will mount using
> the new DSK4, or get rid of the original DSK0 so that it can create a
> new DSK0.
>
Boot to single user mode, use mount -o to remount the root disk in
read write mode, then use dsfmgr to swap dsk0 and dsk4.
Then remove dsk4.
The problem is caused by your 'recovered' root disk having a different
WWID to the old one and so it is seen as a new device.
HTH
Adam




