Dr. David Kirkby wrote:

> I have won an eBay auction for a Dec Alpha 600a, with 512 Mb of RAM
> and a 600 MHz CPU.

Cool!  Looks to me like you got a good deal on it, too!  :-)

> 1) From the information available on the auction site, does it look
>    like that machine will run Tru64 UNIX with no hassles?

I see no reason why it wouldn't, but I can't say for sure whether or not
the graphics and SCSI controllers will be supported by Tru64.  My
suspicion is that they would be, but you might check around
DEC/Compaq/HP's web site to try and find out for sure.  Older systems
will be more difficult to find information on, but I'm pretty sure the
information is still there.

> The CD-ROM drive is IDE, but I have an external SCSI CD-ROM ...

It should run fine with the IDE CD drive, using a pseudo-SCSI driver for
it, (certainly most of the newer AlphaServers we have where I work have
IDE CD drives, and they appear at the OS level as SCSI drives and work
just fine).

> 2) Does one need to use a SCSI CD-ROM drive to boot Tru64, or will it
>    boot from an IDE CD-ROM drive?

In my experience (with different models than what you'll be receiving,
I admit), it'll boot fine with the IDE drive, you won't even notice
the difference.  Depending on what you intend to use the CD drive for,
you may not even need to replace it with a faster one.  (If you only
using it to install the OS and additional software, why bother?)

> 3) Do I need to look for a PowerStorm card?

You may have to check the release notes of the OS version you intend to
use.  I'm really not sure.

> 5) The poster on comp.sys.dec said I would need to switch from
>    AlphaBIOS to SRM. I found several references on the web to this,
>    but would like to know if this is a risky procedure, which can result
>    in a totally dead motherboard, or is pretty harmless? 

It's quite painless (assuming that particular system supports the SRM
console;  I'm not sure if any of the AlphaServer systems don't...) and
you could theoretically switch back and forth a number of times without
any trouble.  There should be a selection in one of the AlphaBIOS's
menus to switch to SRM, (I'm not sure which, as I've never actually used
AlphaBIOS).

> 6) Since the seller says the machine installs Redhat quite happily and
>    is willing to put that on it, does that mean it must already have SRM?

Good question.  You might ask the seller, who no-doubt knows for sure.

> 7) Are there any specific issues to watch out for if wanting to run
>    Tru64 UNIX on this machine? 

Licensing.  I work on DEC/Tru64 Unix systems, but I much prefer the
Linux form of licensing...

