Dr. David Kirkby wrote:

> 1) From the information available on the auction site, does it look
> like that machine will run Tru64 UNIX with no hassles? The CD-ROM
> drive is IDE, but I have an external SCSI CD-ROM that I use with Sun
> machines, so that is not really an issue if one needs a SCSI CD-ROM to
> boot the machine.

The only thing about CD-ROMs on Tru64 to look out for is the fact that Alpha 
needs a CD-ROM that has 512-byte block size. That should be OK with the your 
installed CD-ROM, but not with your external one. Most CD-ROMs I've seen have a 
block size of 2048. I saw a Toshiba that was in one ALpha, it had a switch to 
choose between 512 and 2048 byte block size.

> 2) Does one need to use a SCSI CD-ROM drive to boot Tru64, or will it
> boot from an IDE CD-ROM drive? (I has an external SCSI one, but
> clearly using an internal CD-ROM is easier and much cheaper to get a
> faster model.

It will boot of an IDE, but your problems might be in locating a proper firmware 
upgrade CD, in order to install SRM console.

> 3) From what I gather from someone on comp.sys.dec, the Q-logic SCSI
> controller should be okay for Tru64, but I'm less sure about the
> graphics card, since he said 600au's shipped with PowerStorm graphics
> cards, yet the auction says it has a Cirrus Logic card. Do I need to
> look for a PowerStorm card?

Perhaps, perhaps not.

> 4) What sort of RAM does this Dec Alpha take - do I really need to buy
> Dev/Compaq/HP ram, or will standard PC-100 ram do? What are the
> maximum sizes of DIMMs.

Dunno, but Kingston was a fairly good supplier of memories for Alpha systems.

> 5) The poster on comp.sys.dec said I would need to switch from
> AlphaBIOS to SRM. I found several references on the web to this, but
> would like to know if this is a risky procedure, which can result in a
> totally dead motherboard, or is pretty harmless?

That is basically a firmware upgrade. Power loss during that procedure may 
result in a semi-dead computer (needing a manual EEPROM loading in a repair 
shop). Some models of Alpha (like mine AS-200) had two EEPROM chips and could 
hold both AlphaBIOS and SRM, most of the workstations have only one, so you have 
to choose which one you want to run. For Tru64 you must have SRM, for Windows NT 
- AlphaBIOS, while Linux can use both (but be sure to download and burn images 
for Compaq Linux Starter Kit).

> 6) Since the seller says the machine installs Redhat quite happily and
> is willing to put that on it, does that mean it must already have SRM?
> Some web references say Linux will only run on SRM, but I don't know
> if that information is out of date and perhaps this is not currently
> so.

See above. Linux can use either LILO to boot from AlphaBIOS or (if memory 
serves) SBOOT for SRM, but the easiest way is to get that Compaq Linux Starter 
CD and install a boot manager from it.

> 7) Are there any specific issues to watch out for if wanting to run
> Tru64 UNIX on this machine?

Someone mentioned licenses. Basically, every "advanced" product coming from 
DEC/Comapq/HP will require licenses, some of which are "mucho expencive". Here 
is a small list:

1. base license
    name: OSF-BASE/UNIX-SERVER/UNIX-WORKSTATION
    price: $99-$????
    This is for the base OS. It also activates 2 or 4 implicit users.

2. user license
    name: OSF-USER
    price: depending on the number of users, UNLIMITED is cca. $5,000
    This is for concurent user sessions (telnet, ssh, ftp).

3. Advanced File System Utilities
    name: ADVFS-UTILITIES
    price: $12,000
    This is for some useful utilities for AdvFS (like NTFS on NT).

4. Logical Storage Manger
    name: LSM-??
    price: $11,000
    This a software RAID 0, 1, 1+0, 5

5. DEC C++
    name: CXXOSF-USER
    price: ?
    This is for using DEC's C++ (a very good product)

6. Development Tools
    name: OSF-DEV-USER
    price: ?
    This is for activating debuggers.

> 8) Does anyone have a copy of Tru64 UNIX they wish to sell, preferably
> in the UK, but if its just the CD's, I guess they won't cost much to
> ship?

Well, the CD-ROM kit itself costs about $300, plus an academic/home OSF-BASE 
license $99, it is  $400.

