>>>>> "Oisin" == Oisin McGuinness <oisin@smbc-cm.com> writes:

Oisin> Thanks for the reply, but, I'm sorry, I don't understand all the SAN
Oisin> terminology yet; could you explain what you mean by
Oisin> "transparent failure mode", and "multibus failover mode"?
Oisin> (Or possibly refer me to where these are defined in the documents.)

It's not SAN terminology. It's how the HSG80 controller pair is
implemented. In "multibus failover mode" the controller offers better
performance. However the host needs to be involved to failover a LUN
from one controller to the other controller. In "transparent failover
mode" the performance is potentially less, but failover of a LUN from
one controller to the other happens in a way that is transparent to
the host.

Since V4 can not do dynamic multi-pathing (path failover and load
balancing), the controller must be in "transparent failover mode". The
HSV110 array only does the equivalent of "multibus failover mode".

You can probably connect with V4 to the HSV110, but the configuration
would have several SPOF's (Single Point Of Failures) and nobody has
tested with it. We've always recommended people to upgrade to V5 if
they need to talk to their storage over Fibre Channel.

