Thanks MArtin, I shall try the script. The very helpful folks on the
Tru64 Manager�s mailing list also proposed similar steps. Here is what
I eventually used to get my system running (maybe it will help others
too):

1. boot from 5.1B install CD and restore the dump from tape
2. delete the device databases on the restored system, specifically
dec_devsw_db*, dec_hw_db*, dec_hwc_ldb*, dec_scsi_db* in /etc
3. replace the restored /etc/dfsl.dat with the dfsl.dat from /var/etc
of the booted install CD
4. boot into single user from the restored genvmunix kernel, which
should create new device databases
5. run "dn_setup -init" and "dsfmgr -K" to get rid of old device names
and regenerate new ones
6. mount the non-root filesystems (I preferred to use bcheckrc)
without having to adjust /etc/fstab (now have same old device names)
7. reinstall the generic sysconfigtab either from CD or from
/etc/sysconfigtab.GENERIC
8. rebuild a new kernel using "doconfig" and copy it to /vmunix 
9. boot normally on the restored disk

Other comments I received included the "btcreate" utility which
apparently can create a bootable tape dump which can be more easily
restored. I have not tried this yet though, perhaps someone else can
comment on their experience...

Thanks for the help.

Michael

