I have attempted to follow these instructions and things still just
did not seem to work... see below for more details.
 
> However, your message seems to suggest that you are writing the binary
> data of a structure directly over the network.  Is that correct?  If so,
> you should be aware many people consider this a poor solution because
> it's not very portable.  I always use some intermediate data encoding
> which ensures portable behavior (in my case CORBA, but other data
> encoders exist).

Rather than get into the merits of binary vs. text based protocols,
this is not an open issue for my current effort because I am porting
software that has been around for quite a while, with a significant
installed base. Thus, we need to avoid changing the protocol to
maintain compatibility with the existing installed product.

One example of the structures that I am working with are:

class base {
   long one;
   unsigned short two;
};

class msg : public base {
   char three[size];
};

When used on Tru64, three is offset 6 bytes from the start of the msg
class. For all other platforms this software runs on, three is offset
eight bytes from the start.

I have tried:

class base {
   long one __attribute__ ((aligned(4)));
   unsigned short two __attribute__ ((aligned(4)));
} __attribute__ ((aligned(4)));

class msg : public base {
   char three[size] __attribute__ ((aligned(4)));
} __attribute__ ((aligned(4)));

and get the same result. I have tried just about every permutation I
can think of and continue to get exactly the same result.

I originally thought I would have to pack things on Tru64 since its
natural alignment is 8 byte boundaries. But, the actual results I am
seeing are that these classes are already being packed more tightly
than I want, so that seems the wrong angle to pursue.

The only solution I've found thus far is to manually pad as:

class base {
   long one;
   unsigned short two;
   short pad;
}

This works just fine, though it means I have to manually go through
all of my classes and check their alignment on Tru64, not a trivial
task.

Thanks again for your help with this.

-Marc


