Lukasz Tylutki wrote:

> The best way to get the performace statistics is to use  collect 
> utility.

Although that might be true, you need to be in group "system" in order to
be able to run collect.  This means (once again) getting cooperation from
the sysadmins (and I don't want to imply that we aren't a cooperative
bunch, because in my view we are;  Now if only those darned DBAs would
learn to be! ;-)

I have the impression the original poster is having difficulty obtaining
cooperation from the sysadmins...

You could try monitor (we got the copy we're using from
ftp://vzmaftpdigpro.zma.compaq.com/pub/Digital/monitor.alpha.Z, but I
make no promises), which you'd be able to install without requiring
sysadmin privileges (though I believe it needs to read from /dev/kmem
and/or /dev/mem; there's that darned sysadmin cooperation requirement
again!).  I'm not sure it will tell you as much as collect can, though
this is the tool I normally use, (it will tell you about memory and cpu
usage as well as details of disk activity, though not on a
per-filesystem level; only on a per disk level).  Also, I've never used
it on a cluster (unless you count a stand-alone system as a 1-member
cluster) so I can't vouch for its usefulness in that environment.

Either way, you're likely going to need cooperation from the sysadmins.

To the original poster, keep using vmstat but use iostat as well (you'll
need to list which disks you want iostat to monitor).  You don't need
sysadmin approval to use either of those, and they'll still give you a
fair amount of information.

As Lukasz pointed out, watching free memory and page out will give you
a picture of the memory and swap usage.  If the CPU idle time is high,
but performance seems sluggish, start looking at disk activity;  It's
possible that something is disk-bound or the disk subsystem simply isn't
keeping up with the demand.

If your application is mostly client-server, and you find it does look
as though the server is handling its end reasonably well, it's time to
check the network: use "netstat -d -I interface-name 2" (find the
interface name with "ifconfig -a" if you need to and change the interval
as you see fit).

If the network seems to be keeping up, the disks aren't overloaded, and
the CPU and memory resources aren't running low, but the performance of
the whole system still is not satisfacory, you'll need to look
elsewhere: is there an application server, or forms server?  What about
the client side?

I hope that helps...

