        man advfs
        man mkfdmn
        man addvol
        man mkfset

        mkfdmn /dev/disk/dsk22c domain_name
        addvol /dev/disk/dsk23c domain_name
        addvol /dev/disk/dsk24c domain_name
        mkfset domain_name fileset_name
        mount domain_name#fileset_name /mount_point

where domain_name can be whatever you want to call it.
where fileset_name can be whatever you want to call it.
where each individual volume is less than the 1TB single volume limit.

There are also manuals that describe AdvFS (in print, on CD, and on-line 
<http://www.tru64unix.compaq.com/docs/>).  There is also an AdvFS Quick 
Start web page at 
<http://www.tru64unix.compaq.com/docs/advfs_ref/frame.html>

However, the basic system only allows AdvFS to include 1 volume.  To use 
the addvol command you need the Advanced Utilities license.

If you have the license, then you can add up to 256 volumes to create 
domains as large as 256TB, and individual files can be as large as 16TB.

I am a little behind the curve on exactly what was included in v5.1b, 
but it maybe possible with AdvFS on Tru64 UNIX v5.1b to use 2TB volumes 
so you may not need to use addvol in order to get a 1.2TB file system.  
You might want to check the release notes to see if this was mentioned.

Good luck.

